# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-ccl-executable-drops-log-output — the dumped alfe-ccl prints no alfe[...] log lines
#+STATUS: DONE (clautolisp 2.2.75 / alfe 2.2.75 / read-autolisp 1.9.1, 2026-09-16)
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: alfe-cli
#+DATE: 2026-09-16

* Observed

Found while verifying alfe-installed-runtime-prefix-discovery on
poseidon (Linux x86-64), with the executables that
=make build-alfe-sbcl= / =make build-alfe-ccl= produce at alfe 2.2.74.
Both were run the same way, stdout and stderr sent to separate files:

| command                                                    | alfe-sbcl stderr | alfe-ccl stderr |
|------------------------------------------------------------+------------------+-----------------|
| =--clautolisp -d -x '(princ 1)'=                           | 10 lines         | 0 lines         |
| =--bricscad --debug --print-command -x '(princ 1)'= (1)    | 21 lines         | 0 lines         |
| =--no-such-option=                                         | the error        | the error       |

(1) with =BRICSCAD_EXE=/usr/bin/true=.

So the CCL image does not lose stderr: an error written with
=(format *error-output* ...)= appears. What never appears is output from
the =alfe.logging= helpers (=log-debug=, =log-verbose=, and presumably
=log-warn=): no =alfe[debug]:= line at all, even though the run itself
works. For instance, the prepared workdir is correct.

* Why it matters

=log-warn= is how alfe reports trouble that does not abort the run,
such as the "bootstrap LSP not staged" warnings and the READY-timeout
details. A CCL alfe that fails in the field would print only its final
error line, and =--debug= would show nothing. The shipped Windows alfe
is the SBCL build, which is why this has not been noticed.

* Not yet known

The cause. The logging code is simple: =emit= writes to
=(or *log-stream* *error-output*)= and calls =finish-output=. The level
is set with =set-level= in =cli.lisp= before the first =log-debug=. The
alfe FiveAM suite passes under a non-dumped CCL, so the problem appears
to be specific to the dumped image. Worth checking first:
- whether =*current-level*= really changes in the dumped image;
- whether the =alfe.logging= functions end up writing to a stream
  captured at =save-application= time (CCL rebinds the standard streams
  when the application starts).

* Acceptance

- =alfe-ccl --clautolisp -d -x '(princ 1)'= prints the same
  =alfe[debug]:= lines as =alfe-sbcl=.
- A test runs the BUILT executable, not just the test image, because a
  test image passes today.

* Resolution

** The real cause (the guesses above were wrong)

Logging was never broken. =alfe-ccl -v= logs normally, and so do
warnings. The program never received =-d= or =--debug=:

- Before any Lisp code runs, the CCL kernel (=pmcl-kernel.c=,
  =process_options=) scans the argv of every executable, including a
  =save-application= image with a prepended kernel, and removes its
  own options: =-d --debug -b --batch --no-sigtrap --avx --no-avx=, and
  =-I -R -S -Z= (and their long forms) together with their values. It
  stops only at =--=.
- It checks every argument that starts with =-=, including the values
  given to our own options.

A minimal =save-application= program printing
=ccl:*command-line-argument-list*= showed =-d= and =--debug= missing.
=-d=/=--debug= is a SHARED CLI option, so =clautolisp-ccl= was affected
too.

** Fix — the shared image generator (=autolisp-reader/tools/read-autolisp/generate.lisp=)

The kernel only rewrites its pointer array, and the OS keeps the
original command line. =argv= now reads that command line on CCL:
- =/proc/self/cmdline= (Linux), or =/proc/curproc/cmdline= (FreeBSD
  with procfs);
- on macOS, which has no =/proc=, =sysctl= =KERN_PROCARGS2=.

The OS list replaces the kernel's list only when it has the same
argv[0] and the kernel's list is a subsequence of it. Otherwise the
kernel's list is kept. All three programs built by the generator
(alfe, clautolisp, read-autolisp) get the fix.

** Tests

- =tools/clautolisp/tests/shipped-debug-flag-test.sh EXE ARGS…=:
  =-d= and =--debug= must each add stderr output compared with the same
  run without them. It runs from:
  - =make test-shipped-debug-flag[-ccl]= (clautolisp);
  - =make test-alfe-{sbcl,ccl}-debug-flag= (alfe).

  The =test-shipped-compiler[-ccl]= targets call it, so the existing CI
  jobs =test:clautolisp:compiler:{sbcl,ccl}= (and the GitHub mirror)
  enforce it on the built executables.
- Results:
  - =clautolisp-ccl=: 4 → 365 lines.
  - =clautolisp-sbcl=: 4 → 17.
  - =alfe-ccl= and =alfe-sbcl=: 0 → 10.
- Seen red: with the OS list disabled, the check fails on
  =clautolisp-ccl= with "The flag never reached the program".
- The parsers (NUL-split, =KERN_PROCARGS2=) and the =/proc= read were
  checked under CCL on Linux. There, =argv= returned =--batch= after
  the kernel had removed it.

** Not verified

- The macOS =KERN_PROCARGS2= path. It compiles under CCL with =:darwin=
  added to the features, and its parser is tested on a synthetic
  block, but no CCL on macOS ran it. Anything unexpected falls back to
  the kernel's list, which is the old behaviour.
- Windows: not covered. The kernel removes the same options there, and
  the original command line would come from =GetCommandLineW=. No
  Windows CCL executable is built today.
